    "story": "Her hands don’t stop moving.\n\nNot because she’s calm — but because stopping would mean noticing how fast they keep coming, how thin the margin is between order and collapse. Bandages, instruments, breath counts. A rhythm learned under fire.\n\nSomewhere beyond the canvas walls, the war keeps deciding who arrives. In here, she decides who gets a chance to leave.",
    "notes": "Under Pressure belongs to a body of work concerned with responsibility rather than spectacle. The image draws its strength from compression — limited space, limited time, limited margin for error — and asks the viewer to stay with the moment rather than resolve it.\n\nVisually, the piece echoes the restraint found in mid-century documentary painting and wartime illustration, while its photographic realism anchors it firmly in lived experience. The absence of overt action allows the psychological stakes to carry the frame.\n\nPrinted in the Engrained Series using a five-layer UV process on 1/2\" Baltic Birch, the natural grain subtly interacts with the image’s tonal structure, reinforcing the material weight of the moment. Each piece is produced as a limited edition, signed and archived, intended to endure as both object and narrative artifact.",
